  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 1, $j$1.1 Interpreting Expressions$j$, $j${"body": "Key words: a term is a number, a variable, or a product of both. A coefficient is the number multiplied by a variable. A constant is a term with no variable. Like terms have the same variable part, such as 5x² and −3x².\n\nWorked example: Evaluate 3x² − 2x + 5 when x = 4.\n3(4)² − 2(4) + 5 = 3(16) − 8 + 5 = 48 − 8 + 5 = 45.\n\nWorked example: A gym charges C = 25m + 60, where m is the number of months. The 25 is the monthly cost and the 60 is a one time fee.\n\nThink it through: an expression is a sentence written in math. Read it piece by piece and say what each part does. In 25m + 60, the 25 grows with m, and the 60 never changes. Order of operations rules every evaluation: parentheses, exponents, multiplication and division, then addition and subtraction. A common mistake is to treat 3x² as (3x)². The exponent belongs only to x, so with x = 4 you square 4 first to get 16, then multiply by 3 to get 48. Writing each step on its own line keeps you from skipping one.\n\nYour task: (1) Evaluate 2x² + x − 6 when x = 3. (2) In the expression 7n + 12, name the coefficient and the constant. (3) Write one sentence explaining what each part of C = 15h + 40 means if h is hours and C is the total cost of a repair.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.AR.1.1.", "video": "", "script": "Welcome to Algebra 1. Algebra is the language of patterns, and today we learn to read that language. Think of it as learning the grammar before we write the sentences.\n\nEvery algebraic expression is built from terms. A term is a number, a variable, or a product of numbers and variables. In the expression three x squared minus two x plus five, there are three terms. The number in front of a variable is called the coefficient. So in three x squared, the coefficient is three. A term with no variable, like the five, is called a constant, because its value never changes.\n\nTerms that have exactly the same variable part are called like terms. Five x squared and negative three x squared are like terms, so we can combine them into two x squared. But four x and four x squared are not like terms, because the variable parts are different.\n\nNext, evaluating. To evaluate an expression means to replace the variable with a number and calculate. Follow the order of operations: parentheses first, then exponents, then multiplication and division from left to right, then addition and subtraction from left to right. For example, to evaluate three x squared minus two x plus five when x equals four, we compute four squared first, which is sixteen. Three times sixteen is forty eight. Two times four is eight. Forty eight minus eight plus five is forty five.\n\nNow the most important skill of this lesson: interpreting expressions. Algebra becomes powerful when you can say what each part means in the real world. Suppose a gym charges twenty five dollars per month plus a sixty dollar sign up fee. We can write the total cost as C equals twenty five m plus sixty, where m is the number of months. The coefficient, twenty five, tells us the cost for each month. The constant, sixty, tells us the fee we pay one time, no matter how many months. When you can read an expression like a sentence, you understand it.\n\nHere is a habit I want you to build. Whenever you see an expression, ask three questions. What are the terms? What does each part stand for? And what happens when the variable changes? Those three questions will carry you through the whole course.\n\nYour task today is to evaluate an expression, name a coefficient and a constant, and explain in one sentence what each part of a repair bill formula means. Then take the four question check.\n\nDo not rush. Mathematics rewards patience, and every strong builder starts by learning the tools. Scripture says in Proverbs, a wise man will hear, and will increase learning. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.AR.1.1"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$1.1 Interpreting Expressions$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 2, $j$1.2 Laws of Exponents$j$, $j${"body": "Laws of exponents (a and b not zero):\nProduct: aᵐ · aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ.\nQuotient: aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ.\nPower of a power: (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ.\nPower of a product: (ab)ⁿ = aⁿbⁿ.\nZero exponent: a⁰ = 1.\nNegative exponent: a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ.\nRational exponent: a^(1/2) = √a, and a^(m/n) = the nth root of a, raised to the power m.\n\nWorked examples: x⁴ · x³ = x⁷. (2x³)² = 4x⁶. 2⁻³ = 1/8. 8^(2/3) = (cube root of 8)² = 2² = 4.\n\nThink it through: each law is a shortcut for writing out repeated multiplication. x² · x³ means (x · x)(x · x · x), which is five x factors, so x⁵. That is why you add exponents when you multiply and multiply exponents when you raise a power to a power. Compare x² · x³ = x⁵ with (x²)³ = x⁶. A common mistake is (2x³)² = 2x⁶. The 2 is also squared, so the answer is 4x⁶. A negative exponent does not make the number negative. It flips it: 2⁻³ = 1/8. Test any rule with small numbers: 2² · 2³ = 4 · 8 = 32, and 2⁵ = 32.\n\nYour task: simplify (a) y⁵ · y² (b) (3a²)³ (c) x⁸ ÷ x³ (d) 4⁻² (e) 27^(1/3) (f) 16^(3/2).\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.NSO.1.1, MA.912.NSO.1.2.", "video": "", "script": "Exponents are a shortcut for repeated multiplication. Instead of writing x times x times x times x, we write x to the fourth power. Today we learn the laws that let us simplify exponents quickly and correctly.\n\nLet us begin with the product rule. When you multiply two powers with the same base, you add the exponents. So x to the fourth times x to the third is x to the seventh. Why? Because four x's multiplied by three more x's gives seven x's in all.\n\nThe quotient rule is the opposite. When you divide powers with the same base, you subtract the exponents. x to the eight divided by x to the three equals x to the fifth.\n\nThe power of a power rule says that when you raise a power to another power, you multiply the exponents. Open parenthesis x squared, close parenthesis, cubed, equals x to the sixth. And the power of a product rule says each factor gets the exponent. Two x cubed, all squared, becomes two squared times x to the sixth, which is four x to the sixth. Notice that the two also gets squared. That is a common mistake, so watch for it.\n\nNow the special cases. Any nonzero number to the zero power equals one. This follows from the quotient rule, because x cubed divided by x cubed must equal one, and the rule gives x to the zero. A negative exponent means a reciprocal. Two to the negative three is one over two cubed, which is one eighth. A negative exponent does not make the answer negative. It moves the base to the other side of the fraction bar.\n\nFinally, rational exponents. An exponent of one half means square root. Nine to the one half is three. More generally, an exponent like two thirds means take the cube root, then square. Eight to the two thirds: the cube root of eight is two, and two squared is four.\n\nHere is a quick strategy. Look at the base. If the bases match, use the product and quotient rules. If you see a power raised to a power, multiply. If you see a negative or fractional exponent, rewrite it first.\n\nYour task today is to simplify six expressions using these laws. Then take the four question check.\n\nGod is a God of order, and mathematics reflects that order. As Paul writes, let all things be done decently and in order. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.NSO.1.1, MA.912.NSO.1.2"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$1.2 Laws of Exponents$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 3, $j$1.3 Working with Radicals$j$, $j${"body": "A radical sign (√) asks for a number that, multiplied by itself, gives the number inside. To simplify, pull out perfect squares: √50 = √(25 · 2) = 5√2.\n\nAdd and subtract only like radicals: 3√2 + 2√2 = 5√2. Simplify first: √12 + √27 = 2√3 + 3√3 = 5√3.\n\nMultiply by multiplying the insides: √3 · √12 = √36 = 6. Also (√7)² = 7.\n\nPerfect squares to know: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100, 121, 144.\n\nThink it through: simplifying a radical means finding the largest perfect square hiding inside it. For √72, the largest perfect square factor is 36, so √72 = √36 · √2 = 6√2. You cannot add radicals with different insides, just as you cannot add 2 apples and 3 oranges into 5 apples. A common mistake is writing √2 + √3 = √5. Check with a calculator: 1.414 + 1.732 = 3.146, but √5 is about 2.236. Simplify every radical first, because sometimes unlike looking radicals turn out to be like radicals, as with √12 and √27.\n\nYour task: simplify (a) √72 (b) √18 + √8 (c) √5 · √20 (d) √48 (e) 2√3 · 4√3.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.NSO.1.4.", "video": "", "script": "Today we work with radicals, which are roots. The most common radical is the square root. The square root of a number asks, what number multiplied by itself gives this? The square root of sixty four is eight, because eight times eight equals sixty four.\n\nSome square roots are not whole numbers. The square root of two is about one point four one four, and it never ends. In algebra we often leave the answer in radical form because that is exact. But we do simplify it as far as possible.\n\nHere is the method for simplifying a radical. Find the largest perfect square that divides the number inside. Perfect squares are one, four, nine, sixteen, twenty five, thirty six, and so on. For the square root of fifty, think, fifty equals twenty five times two. The square root of twenty five is five, so we pull the five outside, and the answer is five root two. For the square root of seventy two, thirty six goes into seventy two twice, so the answer is six root two.\n\nNow adding and subtracting. You can only combine radicals that are like, meaning the number inside the root is the same. Three root two plus two root two equals five root two, just like three apples plus two apples equals five apples. But root two plus root three cannot be combined. Sometimes radicals look different but become like after you simplify them. Root eighteen plus root eight: root eighteen is three root two, root eight is two root two, so the sum is five root two.\n\nMultiplying is easier. Multiply the numbers inside the roots and then simplify. Root five times root twenty is root one hundred, which is ten. And any square root squared gives back the number inside, so root seven squared is seven.\n\nA good habit: always check whether your answer can be simplified further. A teacher would call an answer incomplete if a perfect square is still hiding inside the radical.\n\nYour task today is to simplify five radical expressions, including one sum and two products. Then take the four question check.\n\nAs you work, remember that precision matters. In Scripture we are told that a false balance is abomination to the LORD: but a just weight is his delight. Exact answers honor the truth. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.NSO.1.4"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$1.3 Working with Radicals$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 4, $j$1.4 Adding, Subtracting, Multiplying and Dividing Polynomials$j$, $j${"body": "A polynomial is a sum of terms with whole number exponents. To add or subtract, combine like terms. When subtracting, distribute the negative sign to every term in the second polynomial.\n\nWorked example (add): (3x² + 2x − 1) + (x² − 5x + 4) = 4x² − 3x + 3.\nWorked example (subtract): (5x − 3) − (2x − 7) = 5x − 3 − 2x + 7 = 3x + 4.\nWorked example (multiply): (x + 4)(x − 2) = x² − 2x + 4x − 8 = x² + 2x − 8.\nWorked example (divide by a monomial): (12x³ − 8x²) ÷ 4x = 3x² − 2x.\n\nThink it through: add and subtract polynomials by combining like terms only. When you multiply, every term in the first polynomial must meet every term in the second. For (x + 4)(x − 2), the four products are x · x, x · (−2), 4 · x and 4 · (−2). The most common error is a sign mistake when subtracting. In (5x − 3) − (2x − 7), the minus sign changes both terms, so −(−7) becomes +7. You can always check an answer by choosing a value for x. Let x = 1: (1 + 4)(1 − 2) = −5, and x² + 2x − 8 = 1 + 2 − 8 = −5. They match.\n\nYour task: (a) (4x² − x + 7) + (2x² + 3x − 9) (b) (6x − 5) − (x − 8) (c) (x + 5)(x + 3) (d) (2x − 1)(x + 4) (e) (15x⁴ − 10x²) ÷ 5x.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.AR.1.3, MA.912.AR.1.4.", "video": "", "script": "A polynomial is an expression made of terms added together, where each variable has a whole number exponent. Four x squared plus three x minus seven is a polynomial. Today we learn to add, subtract, multiply and divide them.\n\nAdding is simple. Remove the parentheses and combine like terms. Take three x squared plus two x minus one, plus x squared minus five x plus four. The x squared terms give four x squared. The x terms give negative three x. The constants give three. So the sum is four x squared minus three x plus three.\n\nSubtracting is where students slip. The subtraction sign applies to every term in the second polynomial. So five x minus three, minus the quantity two x minus seven, becomes five x minus three minus two x plus seven. Notice that negative seven became positive seven. The answer is three x plus four. A good tip is to change the signs of everything in the second polynomial first, and then add.\n\nMultiplying uses the distributive property. When we multiply two binomials, like x plus four times x minus two, we multiply every term in the first by every term in the second. x times x is x squared. x times negative two is negative two x. Four times x is four x. Four times negative two is negative eight. Combine the like terms, and we have x squared plus two x minus eight. Many students remember this as F O I L, first, outer, inner, last.\n\nTo divide a polynomial by a monomial, divide each term separately. Twelve x cubed minus eight x squared, divided by four x, gives three x squared minus two x.\n\nThese skills have real uses. In the book of Genesis, Noah is told the ark would be three hundred cubits long, fifty cubits wide, and thirty cubits high. Its volume is the product of the three: three hundred times fifty times thirty, or four hundred fifty thousand cubic cubits. When builders use variables for the length, width and height, they multiply polynomials in the same way.\n\nYour task today is to complete five problems covering each operation. Then take the four question check.\n\nTake your time with signs. Most errors in algebra are sign errors, and most sign errors can be caught by slowing down. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.AR.1.3, MA.912.AR.1.4"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$1.4 Adding, Subtracting, Multiplying and Dividing Polynomials$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'assessment', 1, $j$Assessment 1: Expressions and Polynomials, Show Your Work$j$, $j${"prompt": "Show every step. (1) Evaluate 4x² − 5x + 2 when x = 3. (2) Simplify (2a³b)³. (3) Simplify √98 + √32. (4) Subtract (7x² + 3x − 4) − (2x² − x + 6). (5) Multiply (3x − 2)(x + 5). (6) In your own words, explain what each part of C = 12h + 35 means for a mechanic who charges by the hour.", "rubric": "Total 100. Items 1 to 5 are worth 15 each, with 10 for the correct answer and 5 for clear work. Item 6 is worth 25: identifies the coefficient 12 as the hourly rate, identifies the constant 35 as a one time fee, and uses full sentences."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'assessment' and title = $j$Assessment 1: Expressions and Polynomials, Show Your Work$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'discussion', 1, $j$Discussion 1: Where I See Algebra$j$, $j${"prompt": "Name one place in daily life, in ministry, or in a trade where an expression or formula is used. Describe the quantities involved and what each part of the formula means. Respond in 100 to 150 words."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'discussion' and title = $j$Discussion 1: Where I See Algebra$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 1, $j$Check 1.1: Interpreting Expressions$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "In the expression 6x² − 4x + 9, the coefficient of x is:", "options": ["9", "4", "6", "−4"]}, {"q": "Evaluate 2x² − 3x + 1 when x = 3.", "options": ["16", "10", "22", "4"]}, {"q": "A gym charges C = 25m + 60, where m is the number of months. What does 60 represent?", "options": ["The monthly cost", "A one time fee of $60", "The total after 25 months", "The number of months"]}, {"q": "Which pair are like terms?", "options": ["4x and 4x²", "5x² and −3x²", "2xy and 2x", "7 and 7x"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.1: Interpreting Expressions$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{3,1,1,1}'::int[], array[$j$The coefficient of x is the number multiplied by x, which is −4.$j$,$j$2(9) − 9 + 1 = 18 − 9 + 1 = 10.$j$,$j$The constant is the amount paid no matter how many months.$j$,$j$Like terms have exactly the same variable part.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.1: Interpreting Expressions$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 2, $j$Check 1.2: Laws of Exponents$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "Simplify x⁴ · x³.", "options": ["x¹", "x⁷", "x⁴³", "x¹²"]}, {"q": "Simplify (2x³)².", "options": ["2x⁶", "2x⁵", "4x⁶", "4x⁵"]}, {"q": "What is 2⁻³?", "options": ["1/8", "8", "−6", "−8"]}, {"q": "What is 8^(2/3)?", "options": ["16/3", "2", "64", "4"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.2: Laws of Exponents$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{1,2,0,3}'::int[], array[$j$Add the exponents: 4 + 3 = 7.$j$,$j$Square the 2 and multiply the exponents: 4x⁶.$j$,$j$A negative exponent means reciprocal: 1/2³ = 1/8.$j$,$j$The cube root of 8 is 2, and 2² = 4.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.2: Laws of Exponents$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 3, $j$Check 1.3: Working with Radicals$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "Simplify √72.", "options": ["6√2", "3√6", "36√2", "9√2"]}, {"q": "Simplify √18 + √8.", "options": ["5√2", "4√5", "6√2", "√26"]}, {"q": "Simplify √5 · √20.", "options": ["10", "√25", "25", "5√4"]}, {"q": "What is (√7)²?", "options": ["3.5", "14", "√49", "7"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.3: Working with Radicals$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{0,0,0,3}'::int[], array[$j$72 = 36 · 2, so √72 = 6√2.$j$,$j$√18 = 3√2 and √8 = 2√2, and 3√2 + 2√2 = 5√2.$j$,$j$√100 = 10.$j$,$j$Squaring a square root gives the number inside.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.3: Working with Radicals$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 4, $j$Check 1.4: Adding, Subtracting, Multiplying and Dividing Polynomials$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "Add: (3x² + 2x − 1) + (x² − 5x + 4).", "options": ["4x² − 3x + 3", "4x² + 7x + 3", "3x² − 3x + 3", "4x² − 3x − 5"]}, {"q": "Subtract: (5x − 3) − (2x − 7).", "options": ["3x − 4", "3x − 10", "3x + 4", "7x − 10"]}, {"q": "Multiply: (x + 4)(x − 2).", "options": ["x² + 6x − 8", "x² + 2x − 8", "x² − 8", "x² + 2x + 8"]}, {"q": "Divide: (12x³ − 8x²) ÷ 4x.", "options": ["3x³ − 2x²", "3x² − 8x²", "3x² − 2x", "8x² − 4x"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.4: Adding, Subtracting, Multiplying and Dividing Polynomials$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{0,2,1,2}'::int[], array[$j$Combine like terms: 4x², −3x, +3.$j$,$j$5x − 3 − 2x + 7 = 3x + 4.$j$,$j$x² − 2x + 4x − 8 = x² + 2x − 8.$j$,$j$Divide each term by 4x.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 1.4: Adding, Subtracting, Multiplying and Dividing Polynomials$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 5, $j$Unit Quiz 1: Expressions, Exponents, Radicals and Polynomials$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "Evaluate 3x² − x when x = 2.", "options": ["8", "4", "12", "10"]}, {"q": "Simplify (3x)².", "options": ["6x²", "9x", "9x²", "3x²"]}, {"q": "Simplify √48.", "options": ["6√2", "4√3", "12√4", "2√24"]}, {"q": "What is 16^(1/2)?", "options": ["32", "8", "1/16", "4"]}, {"q": "Multiply: (2x + 3)(x + 1).", "options": ["2x² + 5x + 3", "3x + 4", "2x² + 4x + 3", "2x² + 3"]}, {"q": "Simplify x⁹ ÷ x⁴.", "options": ["x", "x³⁶", "x⁵", "x¹³"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 1: Expressions, Exponents, Radicals and Polynomials$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{3,2,1,3,0,2}'::int[], array[$j$3(4) − 2 = 10.$j$,$j$Both factors are squared.$j$,$j$48 = 16 · 3, so √48 = 4√3.$j$,$j$An exponent of 1/2 means square root.$j$,$j$2x² + 2x + 3x + 3 = 2x² + 5x + 3.$j$,$j$Subtract the exponents: 9 − 4 = 5.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 1: Expressions, Exponents, Radicals and Polynomials$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
